{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7UN4wsuMrAygVDqHENjus","type":"tool_result","content":"ok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/scanner\t(cached)\n/opt/homebrew/bin/jq\n2:13:- **Scanners** — every X (default 4) hours; find problems, file or update issues, close what is fixed\n4:26:    S[\"Scanners<br/>every X h; dedupe by fingerprint, group up to N\"] -->|ard:pending| T[\"Triage<br/>lead routes to PM, architect, designer, docs, eng\"]\n7:72:| `ard:scanner:<name>` | Scanner | Which scanner owns the issue; used for dedupe and backpressure |\n8:73:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label |\n11:156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n12:166:## Scanners\n13:192:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\n14:204:| Scanner | Engine | Every | Group size | Auto-close |\n17:265:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n22:428:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts and 3 review rounds. Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2b1a180-2848-46b9-8210-99eda41e74a1","uuid":"84e65afb-7681-4b63-bdd6-827f0e5d7ead","timestamp":"2026-11-23T19:36:23.700Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/scanner\t(cac